**Terms fixed at closing.** A Home Equity Conversion Mortgage (HECM) loan is boarded with the terms fixed at closing:

- the youngest borrower was at least the plan's minimum age;
- the rate type is one of the plan's permitted rate types;
- the maximum claim amount is the lesser of the appraised value and the FHA maximum claim amount limit;
- the upfront premium was the plan's upfront premium rate, as a percent of the maximum claim amount;
- the origination fee was the plan's rate on the first 200000.00 of the maximum claim amount plus the plan's rate on the rest, never below the plan's smallest origination fee nor above its largest.

**Initial disbursement limit.** The initial disbursement limit is the greater of the plan's initial disbursement percentage of the initial principal limit and the mandatory obligations plus the plan's cushion percentage of the initial principal limit, each percentage figure rounded half up to the cent, and never more than the initial principal limit. On the HECM Fixed-Rate Single Disbursement the whole available amount is paid at closing, per the plan's single full draw rule.

*Worked example (hypothetical inputs, 60.00 and 10.00 percent):* an initial principal limit of 200000.00 with mandatory obligations of 90000.00 gives 120000.00 against 90000.00 plus 20000.00, that is 110000.00; the greater is 120000.00. With mandatory obligations of 115000.00 the alternative is 135000.00, which becomes the limit.

**Servicing fee option.** The loan's servicing fee option is one of the plan's offered options and never changes after closing.

**Rate adjustment.** A monthly adjustable rate resets on the first day of every month to the plan's monthly index plus the loan's margin, never more than the plan's monthly-adjustable lifetime cap above the initial note rate. An annual adjustable rate resets on each closing anniversary to the plan's annual index plus the margin, moving at most the plan's periodic cap at one reset and never more than the plan's annual-adjustable lifetime cap above the initial note rate. A fixed rate never changes, and no rate falls below the margin. A rate adjustment is recorded only while the loan status is active, due and payable or deferral period.
